<section class="slide slide--why" id="why">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="section-label reveal">The reasoning</p>
    <h2 class="section-title reveal reveal-delay-1">Why Are They Doing This?</h2>
    <p class="why-precedent reveal reveal-delay-2">The CA/Browser Forum&rsquo;s rules govern public CAs &mdash; but the <strong>reasoning</strong> applies to every certificate in your organisation.</p>

    <div class="why-reasons">
      <div class="why-reason" data-reason="1">
        <div class="reason-num">01</div>
        <h3>Shorter Exposure Window</h3>
        <p>A compromised key is dangerous for less time. Shorter lifetimes limit the blast radius of any key compromise.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="why-reason" data-reason="2">
        <div class="reason-num">02</div>
        <h3>Forces Automation</h3>
        <p>Human-dependent processes fail at scale. Short lifetimes make automation a requirement, not an option.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="why-reason" data-reason="3">
        <div class="reason-num">03</div>
        <h3>Crypto Agility</h3>
        <p>If an algorithm is broken tomorrow, how quickly can your fleet rotate? Short-lived certs mean faster response.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="why-reason" data-reason="4">
        <div class="reason-num">04</div>
        <h3>Less Revocation Pressure</h3>
        <p>Revocation becomes a bottleneck at scale. Short-lived certs reduce dependency on CRL and OCSP &mdash; they expire before revocation becomes critical.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="why-precedent reveal reveal-delay-7">The pattern is clear: <strong>public standard today &rarr; internal best practice tomorrow.</strong> TLS everywhere, encryption at rest, mutual TLS in service meshes &mdash; all followed this path. The only question is whether you adopt on your terms or wait for an auditor to ask.</p>
  </div>
</section>
